func Count(f machine.Frame[Order]) Order {
  seen, _, err := f.Load(processed)
  if err != nil {
    panic(err)
  }
  if err := f.Save(processed, seen+1); err != nil {
    panic(err)
  }
  f.Set(attempt, f.Get(attempt)+1)

  out := f.Value()
  out.Seen = seen + 1
  return out
}

func Report(f machine.Frame[Order]) Order {
  v := f.Value()
  if v.Seen == 3 {
    Emit(v.Seen)
  }
  return v
}

flow e2e
note """
The end-to-end fixture. It exists to prove the ticket's central claim by RUNNING:
a compiled flow builds and runs against the machine runtime. Every construct here
is one the generator lowers, and the sink prints a locked token from its own func
body so the assertion is on the PROGRAM's stdout rather than on the harness.

IT CARRIES NO checkpoint OR idempotent. Both clauses lower now, but whether this
fixture should gain a checkpointed node is a decision that belongs with the
ruling rather than with the fixture; a checkpoint would also pull a journal, and
with it a raft dependency, into a test whose subject is the builder path.
"""

state {
  processed int
}
var attempt int
source ingest Feed()
transform count Count from ingest
  reads attempt, processed  writes attempt, processed
branch route Interesting from count -> kept, dropped
tee split from kept -> reported, audited
sink emit Report from reported
sink audit Audit from audited
drop dropped
